),(7,4). But (6,3) not in T_7 because (6,3) sum = 9 > 8? Actually n=7 => a+b ≤ 8. (6,3) sum = 9 > 8, not in T_7. So line y = x - 3 covers (4,1),(5,2). It also passes through (6,3) not in T_7, (7,4) not in T_7. So L1 = y = x - 3 covers (4,1),(5,2). Good.\n\nLine y = x - 2 passes through (4,2),(5,3),(6,4),(7,5). (5,3) sum = 8, okay. (6,4) sum = 10 >8, not in T_7. So L2 = y = x - 2 covers (4,2),(5,3). Good.\n\nLine y = x - 1 passes through (4,3),(5,4),(6,5),(7,6). (4,3) sum = 7, okay. (5,4) sum = 9 >8, not in T_7. So L3 = y = x - 1 covers (4,3). Only one point.\n\nLine y = x passes through (4,4),(5,5),(6,6),(7,7). (4,4) sum = 8, okay. (5,5) sum = 10 >8, not in T_7. So L4 = y = x covers (4,4). So far we have covered all points in column 4: (4,1) by L1, (4,2) by L2, (4,3) by L3, (4,4) by L4. Column 5: points (5,1),(5,2),(5,3). (5,2) covered by L1? Actually L1 passes through (5,2). Yes. (5,3) covered by L2. (5,1) remains uncovered. Column 6: points (6,1),(6,2). None covered yet. Column 7: (7,1) remains uncovered.\n\nWe have used all 4 lines. We cannot add more lines. So we need to assign (5,1),(6,1),(6,2),(7,1) to the existing lines. Let's see if any of the lines also pass through those points.\n\n- L1: y = x - 3. At x=5, y=2 (covered). At x=6, y=3 (not in T_7). At x=7, y=4 (not in T_7). So L1 does not cover (5,1),(6,1),(6,2),(7,1).\n\n- L2: y = x - 2. At x=5, y=3 (covered). At x=6, y=4 (not in T_7). At x=7, y=5 (not in T_7). So L2 does not cover those.\n\n- L3: y = x - 1. At x=5, y=4 (not in T_7). At x=6, y=5 (not). At x=7, y=6 (not). So no.\n\n- L4: y = x. At x=5, y=5 (not). At x=6, y=6 (not). At x=7, y=7 (not). So none.\n\nThus we cannot cover the remaining points. So this particular assignment fails.\n\nMaybe we can use lines with different slopes, not just slope 1. Let's try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points. This is a combinatorial design problem. Let's try to think systematically.\n\nWe have 10 points to cover with 4 lines. Each line can intersect each column at most once. So each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So we need to assign points to lines such that each line gets exactly 4 points (or some lines get fewer). Since we have 10 points, the distribution could be (4,3,2,1) or (4,3,3,0) etc. But each line must be sunny (slope not 0, ∞, -1). Let's try to find a configuration.\n\nOne approach: Use lines that pass through points in each column, maybe using slopes that vary. For each line, we need to pick at most one point from each column. So we can think of constructing a 4x4 matrix where rows are lines, columns are columns (4,5,6,7). Each entry is either a point (c, y) or empty. The points must be distinct across the matrix (no point appears twice). And each row must be collinear.\n\nWe can try to assign points to lines as follows:\n\n- L1: (4,1), (5,2), (6,3?), (7,4?) but (6,3) not allowed. So maybe L1: (4,1), (5,2), (6,2?), (7,1?). Let's see if we can find a line passing through (4,1) and (5,2). Slope = (2-1)/(5-4) = 1. So line y = x - 3. At x=6, y=3 (not allowed). At x=7, y=4 (not allowed). So L1 cannot also cover (6,2) or (7,1). So L1 covers only (4,1) and (5,2). That's okay.\n\n- L2: maybe (4,2), (5,1), (6,2?), (7,1?). Let's see line through (4,2) and (5,1): slope = (1-2)/(5-4) = -1, not allowed. So can't.\n\n- L2: (4,2), (5,3), (6,2?), (7,1?). Let's see line through (4,2) and (5,3): slope = (3-2)/(5-4) = 1. So line y = x - 2. At x=6, y=4 (not allowed). So L2 covers (4,2),(5,3).\n\n- L3: (4,3), (6,1?), (7,1?). Let's see line through (4,3) and (6,1): slope = (1-3)/(6-4) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So can't.\n\n- L3: (4,3), (5,1), (6,2?), (7,1?). Let's see line through (4,3) and (5,1): slope = (1-3)/(5-4) = -2, allowed. Equation: y - 3 = -2 (x-4) => y = -2x + 8 + 3 = -2x + 11. At x=6, y = -12 + 11 = -1 (not allowed). At x=7, y = -14 + 11 = -3. So L3 covers (4,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\n- L4: (4,4), (6,1?), (7,1?). Let's see line through (4,4) and (6,1): slope = (1-4)/(6-4) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed. 